#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101751} :activity/id #uuid "a84331d6-8675-50fc-847e-1a6fc6452ad8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049550 :topic/id #uuid "fea8ef8f-fa88-5474-8a11-469807c11031" :topic/math-academy-id 1245} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101919 :tutorial/content "What is the limit of the sequence $a_n$ given by\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n}{2^n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\\, ?\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator grow without bound as $n\\to\\infty.$ However, we notice that for large values of $n,$ we get\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n}{2^n} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{a fairly big number}}{\\textrm{an enormous number}}.\n$$\n\nThe gap between the numerator and denominator continues to grow as $n\\to\\infty.$ So, the limit of the sequence is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n = 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the sequence converges to zero. \n\nWhile this is not a rigorous argument, it's a handy technique that can help us to identify whether specific sequences converge or diverge.\n\nRecall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn! \\gg e^{n} \\gg n^m \\gg \\ln(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gg$ means **much greater than**, and $m\\gt 0.$ We can use this to solve a variety of problems involving limits at infinity." :tutorial/id #uuid "11bd1f5f-efe3-56f1-8a6c-403777c960ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1154 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "baaa1543-36fe-5bdf-a4bd-8fbd22f3864f" :step/math-academy-id 2272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100755 :question/id #uuid "25e1588c-860d-5285-8616-219770b157e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43f839b4-2b0e-5ab5-809a-741747f1016d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049983}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759218610